<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<xri version="1.0">
   <description>
      <p>astro-pi — unified PixInsight distribution (scarter4work)</p>
      <p>NukeX integration/stretching module + EZ Stretch BSC script suite.</p>
      <p>Repository: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/scarter4work/astro-pi/main/repository/</p>
   </description>
   <platform os="linux" arch="x64" version="1.8.0:2.0.0">
      <package fileName="20260709-linux-x64-NukeX.tar.gz" sha1="e7b88ba26fc0a9b9b4eeb19d1c05ec4d93baeec1" type="module" releaseDate="20260709">
         <title>NukeX</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>NukeX 5.1.0.2</b>
            </p>
            <p>v5.1.0.2: Phase B batch sizing is now bounded by available system RAM (not just GPU VRAM), fixing an out-of-memory kill on hosts where RAM is smaller than VRAM headroom (e.g. a 16 GB GPU on a 30 GB machine). Per-batch host footprint is now shown in the Phase B log.</p>
            <p>Astrophotography integration and stretching module. v5.1.0 is a color-science overhaul: physically-grounded, QE-database-driven channel decomposition for OSC/mono and dual-narrowband workflows (fixes the v4 green cast), with per-channel frame accounting for heterogeneous-geometry batches. The QE database is compiled into the module, so it installs with no extra files.</p>
            <p>v5.1.0.1: broadband / clear filter-wheel slot labels (Full, Clear, None, Open, L-Pro, L-QEF, UV/IR-cut) on Bayer frames now route as plain OSC instead of hard-aborting the stack; corrected stale &quot;v4&quot; labels in the UI to v5.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
   </platform>
   <platform os="all" arch="noarch" version="1.8.0:2.0.0">
      <package fileName="EZStretch_v1.0.10.zip" sha1="2714c4063e74284c94b7acdd6d5c84d3ea0e8a71" type="script" releaseDate="20260709">
         <title>EZ Stretch</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>EZ Stretch v1.0.10</b>
            </p>
            <p>Unified stretch suite combining three algorithms in a single interface.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
      <package fileName="EZDonutRepair_v1.0.3.zip" sha1="5995dfccb88861eb6e32f80137bb535b0f61e1a6" type="script" releaseDate="20260709">
         <title>EZ Donut Repair</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>EZ Donut Repair v1.0.3</b>
            </p>
            <p>Removes donut/dust artifacts from astrophotography frames.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
      <package fileName="EZHazeKill_v1.0.1.zip" sha1="6c3033477f590f2bbc6c43df91207570ea0ac016" type="script" releaseDate="20260709">
         <title>EZ Haze Kill</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>EZ Haze Kill v1.0.1</b>
            </p>
            <p>Gradient and haze reduction.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
      <package fileName="gaia-depth-grade_v1.0.12.zip" sha1="b75a6ee5f13e46f201093bc0b1b60955d00b49b1" type="script" releaseDate="20260709">
         <title>Gaia Depth Grade</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>Gaia Depth Grade v1.0.12</b>
            </p>
            <p>Physically-grounded depth grade for astrophotographs using Gaia distances. Defaults to the offline local Gaia DR3 database (the one PixInsight uses for plate-solving); an online Bailer-Jones option is available. On first run it fetches a frozen Python sidecar (~95 MB, linux-x64) from GitHub Releases and verifies it by SHA-256. Requires StarXTerminator.</p>
            <p>v1.0.12 (sidecar 1.0.7): enlarged (near) stars now wear a realistic STAR-SHINE HALO. The halo&apos;s radial shape is stacked from the frame&apos;s own bright, unsaturated, isolated stars and tinted to each star&apos;s own colour, with amplitude anchored to its real peak brightness — replacing the achromatic Gaussian blob that read as fake. Frames with too few clean reference stars fall back to a Moffat profile (with a warning). First run re-fetches the ~95 MB sidecar.</p>
            <p>v1.0.11 (sidecar 1.0.6): adds optional NEBULA DEPTH. A new checkbox grades the starless layer too — dark dust/globules come forward, the diffuse glow recedes, with gentle atmospheric perspective and noise-cored structural relief. The spatial variation comes from the nebula&apos;s own structure; the overall strength is calibrated by the Gaia matched-star distance spread, so the nebula depth matches the stars. Opt-in (off by default) with live Nebula depth / atmospheric / structure sliders.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
   </platform>
</xri>
